Bisect {AB} at the point {E}.  Describe {EBFG} similar and similarly situated to {D|Dt} on {EB}, and complete the parallelogram {AG}. [!just I.9; VI.18]

If then {AG} equals {C|Ct}, that which was proposed is done, for the parallelogram {AG} equal to the given rectilinear figure {C|Ct} has been applied to the given straight line {AB} but falling short by a parallelogram {GB} similar to {D|Dt}.

But, if not, let {HE} be greater than {C|Ct}.

Now {HE} equals {GB}, therefore {GB} is also greater than {C|Ct}.

Construct {KLMN} equal to {GB} minus {C|Ct} and similar and similarly situated to {D|Dt}. [!just VI.25]

But {D|Dt} is similar to {GB}, therefore {KM} is also similar to {GB}. [!just VI.21]

Let, then, {KL} correspond to {GE}, and {LM} to {GF}.

Now, since {GB} equals {C|Ct} and {KM}, therefore {GB} is greater than {KM}, therefore also {GE} is greater than {KL}, and {GF} than {LM}.

Make {GO} equal to {KL}, and {GP} equal to {LM}, and let the parallelogram {OGPQ} be completed, therefore it is equal and similar to {KM}. [!just VI.21; VI.26]

Therefore {GQ} is also similar to {GB},  therefore {GQ} is about the same diameter with {GB}.

Let {GQB} be their diameter, and describe the figure.

Then, since {BG|EBFG} equals {C|Ct} and {KM}, and in them {GQ} equals {KM}, therefore the remainder, the gnomon {UWV|UWVg}, equals the remainder {C|Ct}.

And, since {PR} equals {OS}, add {QB} to each, therefore the whole {PB} equals the whole {OB}.

But {OB} equals {TE}, since the side {AE} also equals the side {EB},  therefore {TE} also equals {PB}. [!just I.36]

Add {OS} to each.  Therefore the whole {TS} equals the whole, the gnomon {VWU|UWVg}.

But the gnomon {VWU|UWVg} was proved equal to {C|Ct}, therefore {TS} also equals {C|Ct}.

Therefore there the parallelogram {ST} equal to the given rectilinear figure {C|Ct} has been applied to the given straight line {AB} but falling short by a parallelogram {QB} similar to {D|Dt}.

<div class="qed">Q.E.F.</div>
----
guide:

When this proposition is used, the given parallelgram {D|Dt} usually is a square.  Then the problem is to cut the line {AB} at a point {S} so that the rectangle {AS} by {SB} equals the given rectilinear figure {C|Ct}.  This special case can be proved with the help of the propositions in Book II.  See the [Guide](/elements/books/bookII/propositions/propII5/#guide) to proposition II.5 for more details.

#### The outline of a simplified proof for rectangles

